1
00:00:06,739 --> 00:00:13,985
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a confusion around emotions and feelings.

2
00:00:14,665 --> 00:00:15,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Feelings, emotions.

3
00:00:16,747 --> 00:00:21,891
[SPEAKER_00]: And people sometimes wonder, what is the Pali word for emotion?

4
00:00:22,960 --> 00:00:29,643
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's... and some translators translate the word citta, the Pali word citta, as emotion.

5
00:00:29,883 --> 00:00:30,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, really?

6
00:00:30,384 --> 00:00:31,384
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's the heart.

7
00:00:32,024 --> 00:00:32,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, right.

8
00:00:32,565 --> 00:00:34,245
[SPEAKER_00]: So, my emotions.

9
00:00:34,285 --> 00:00:34,586
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

10
00:00:34,646 --> 00:00:41,529
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the emotional being, the mind, but... Yeah, it's a bit of a stretch.

11
00:00:41,629 --> 00:00:42,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

12
00:00:43,530 --> 00:00:47,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no, the the the emotions, I mean, based on its own

13
00:00:49,842 --> 00:00:53,560
[SPEAKER_01]: Etymology just means, like, intense feeling.

14
00:00:53,581 --> 00:00:54,606
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, you're emotional.

15
00:00:56,772 --> 00:00:59,232
[SPEAKER_01]: Emotionally positive, emotionally negative.

16
00:00:59,553 --> 00:01:05,314
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it is a kind of mind state, but on the basis of how you feel, just amplified, so to speak.

17
00:01:06,074 --> 00:01:21,236
[SPEAKER_01]: But also, I think, if you if you if you were to ask psychotherapy or so on, I think things got extremely convoluted there, whereby emotions are subconscious, unconscious, physiological, and it's just like something's unconscious.

18
00:01:21,276 --> 00:01:22,737
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you talk about it consciously?

19
00:01:23,397 --> 00:01:24,237
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you even know it?

20
00:01:25,057 --> 00:01:26,618
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's just the amplified feelings.

21
00:01:27,598 --> 00:01:40,344
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you cultivate, see, your responses to feelings, you're basically cultivating an attitude of being emotional, more and more.

22
00:01:40,704 --> 00:01:45,026
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you need to keep giving in to your feelings, in order to become...

23
00:01:45,066 --> 00:01:46,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Eventually, then, you don't have to.

24
00:01:46,487 --> 00:01:47,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Emotions will be automatic.

25
00:01:47,627 --> 00:01:50,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Those mental states will be instantaneous.

26
00:01:50,268 --> 00:01:54,110
[SPEAKER_01]: When touched by unpleasant feeling, or when touched by pleasant feeling, because you cultivated it.

27
00:01:55,075 --> 00:01:57,496
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it doesn't matter the complexity of an emotion.

28
00:01:57,816 --> 00:01:58,917
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always within the feeling.

29
00:01:59,757 --> 00:02:04,800
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always... Oh, how is this emotional, complex, subconsciously, unconscious state felt?

30
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:07,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Consciously, it's felt pleasant.

31
00:02:08,402 --> 00:02:09,242
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the doorway.

32
00:02:09,742 --> 00:02:15,165
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the framework for any emotion, any direction you can take, and any action you can do on account of it.

33
00:02:15,725 --> 00:02:19,647
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't need to worry about this emotion or that emotion.

34
00:02:19,667 --> 00:02:21,228
[SPEAKER_01]: You just need to see whether your actions

35
00:02:22,985 --> 00:02:26,848
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for example, like the satipaṭṭhāna, he knows a lustful mind as a lustful mind.

36
00:02:26,888 --> 00:02:28,769
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could say he knows an emotional

37
00:02:50,610 --> 00:02:51,951
[SPEAKER_00]: Emotionally shaped mind?

38
00:02:52,011 --> 00:02:52,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you could say.

39
00:02:53,652 --> 00:03:00,798
[SPEAKER_01]: But basically, even that, any state of mind, it's underlined by a certain way it's felt.

40
00:03:01,599 --> 00:03:05,262
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you say, he knows elated mind is elated mind, depressed mind is depressed mind.

41
00:03:05,342 --> 00:03:06,703
[SPEAKER_01]: How is elated mind felt?

42
00:03:06,883 --> 00:03:09,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it elated in a positive sense or a negative sense?

43
00:03:10,806 --> 00:03:16,631
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, contemplation of the mind is inseparable from contemplation of the feeling, or thoughts, or the body for that matter.

44
00:03:16,771 --> 00:03:17,872
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's satipaṭṭhāna.

45
00:03:19,534 --> 00:03:24,164
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the four foundations, like one, one and a half.

46
00:03:24,645 --> 00:03:25,346
[SPEAKER_01]: But the point is...

47
00:03:27,267 --> 00:03:36,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever for you designation, word, term, emotion means, you just want to see past it, as in see beyond it, as how it's simultaneously felt.

48
00:03:37,312 --> 00:03:38,012
[SPEAKER_01]: It's emotional.

49
00:03:38,032 --> 00:03:49,297
[SPEAKER_01]: You already know whether it's pleasant or unpleasant, whether you want it to stay longer, whether you're riding the wave of pleasure, excitement, or you're basically absolutely trying to get rid of it at all costs.

50
00:03:49,397 --> 00:03:51,598
[SPEAKER_01]: You're emotionally disturbed, distraught.

51
00:03:51,838 --> 00:03:53,999
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just to say, I feel angry.

52
00:03:54,379 --> 00:03:55,360
[SPEAKER_00]: I have the emotion.

53
00:03:55,801 --> 00:03:57,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can say emotion of anger.

54
00:03:57,302 --> 00:03:57,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Anger.

55
00:03:57,482 --> 00:03:57,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

56
00:03:58,482 --> 00:03:59,762
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm emotionally angry.

57
00:03:59,802 --> 00:04:00,403
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

58
00:04:00,443 --> 00:04:00,983
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what do I do?

59
00:04:01,523 --> 00:04:04,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you stop trying to get rid of the pain of that anger, of that emotion.

60
00:04:05,604 --> 00:04:10,926
[SPEAKER_00]: But what about if I just am aware that I'm angry?

61
00:04:11,046 --> 00:04:13,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I suppose that's the first step, but that won't do anything.

62
00:04:13,387 --> 00:04:14,727
[SPEAKER_01]: That'll just make you aware of the anger.

63
00:04:15,087 --> 00:04:19,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you need to become aware, self-aware of whatever you're feeling, whatever you state in.

64
00:04:20,989 --> 00:04:23,290
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to become aware of the problem in order to deal the problem.

65
00:04:24,097 --> 00:04:28,881
[SPEAKER_01]: But being aware of the problem does not do anything for the problem.

66
00:04:29,321 --> 00:04:37,468
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm aware of the problem, but I need to be more aware of the problem, so that I can actually see the way out of the problem.

67
00:04:37,768 --> 00:04:39,610
[SPEAKER_01]: More often than not, that's the case, yeah.

68
00:04:39,730 --> 00:04:42,012
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to be more aware of the problem.

69
00:04:42,672 --> 00:04:44,554
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to make it a bigger... like a recognition.

70
00:04:44,574 --> 00:04:51,660
[SPEAKER_01]: This is a serious problem, because otherwise you will not master enough determination, effort to keep drilling in that direction and undo it.

71
00:04:52,923 --> 00:04:56,664
[SPEAKER_01]: It's easier to forget about, especially when that emotion has subsided.

72
00:04:57,624 --> 00:05:01,004
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the Buddha, the divine messenger, is something you need to reflect on.

73
00:05:02,145 --> 00:05:10,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Before you get sick, before you get old, and before you're dead, you need to recognize that you are liable to be sick, liable.

74
00:05:10,846 --> 00:05:15,567
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm liable to lust, I'm liable to anger.

75
00:05:16,447 --> 00:05:16,767
[SPEAKER_01]: That's...

76
00:05:16,787 --> 00:05:22,268
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to recognize before these things occur, so basically, so I can uproot them, so they don't occur anymore.

77
00:05:23,985 --> 00:05:29,068
[SPEAKER_01]: But yeah, in order to recognize that you're liable to these things, the first step is to be aware of them when they're present.

78
00:05:29,508 --> 00:05:36,711
[SPEAKER_01]: But thinking that just by being aware when they're present, you're somehow dealing with it, will then not make you think about it when those things are not present.

79
00:05:37,452 --> 00:05:38,152
[SPEAKER_01]: You think you're fine.

80
00:05:38,732 --> 00:05:39,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Because now I'm not angry, see?

81
00:05:41,353 --> 00:05:43,895
[SPEAKER_01]: But is it because you uprooted the basis for anger?

82
00:05:44,255 --> 00:05:48,797
[SPEAKER_01]: But simply because the circumstances are currently soothing you, and going the way you want them to be.

83
00:05:49,417 --> 00:05:52,879
[SPEAKER_00]: Now you're emotionally... you're getting emotional about that.

84
00:05:53,645 --> 00:05:54,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

85
00:05:54,345 --> 00:05:59,307
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you would still be disturbed on some account if things were to change, and they will have to change.

86
00:06:00,407 --> 00:06:03,749
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what about that idea that I brought up earlier, Evers?

87
00:06:05,069 --> 00:06:07,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Emotions shape the mind.

88
00:06:09,778 --> 00:06:25,050
[SPEAKER_00]: Emotion is the shape of the mind, meaning that it actually constricts the mind, it clouds the mind, because it's given now a shape which is twisted and, you know, it's twisted in this particular way.

89
00:06:25,710 --> 00:06:26,671
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of wrong.

90
00:06:26,691 --> 00:06:27,973
[SPEAKER_01]: It lacks perspective.

91
00:06:28,113 --> 00:06:32,658
[SPEAKER_01]: It sort of has stunted the perspective.

92
00:06:33,258 --> 00:06:43,769
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by removing the root cause... For the emotion, you will then remove the root cause for the reduction of your perspective caused by the emotion.

93
00:06:44,250 --> 00:06:46,853
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're emotional, you know...

94
00:06:47,703 --> 00:06:56,970
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you... so, you're no longer emotional, you have a larger perspective, no more narrowed response or automatic response to how you feel.

95
00:07:00,213 --> 00:07:02,775
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no shape then.

96
00:07:02,855 --> 00:07:05,997
[SPEAKER_00]: That mind is no longer constricted.

97
00:07:06,577 --> 00:07:06,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Well...

98
00:07:08,314 --> 00:07:13,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, the mind is not only constricted, but more like you are not constricted in regards to the shape of the mind.

99
00:07:14,635 --> 00:07:18,836
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind still will have some shape, for as long as the mind is there.

100
00:07:18,956 --> 00:07:21,597
[SPEAKER_01]: But the certain shapes will not constrict the mind anymore.

101
00:07:23,057 --> 00:07:25,738
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how you liberate your understanding.

102
00:07:25,758 --> 00:07:27,198
[SPEAKER_01]: But your understanding liberates you.

103
00:07:28,478 --> 00:07:34,339
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in satipaṭṭhāna, you know depressed, constricted, restricted mind, as depressed, constricted, restricted mind.

104
00:07:34,439 --> 00:07:36,880
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are not depressed, constricted and restricted by it.

105
00:07:38,896 --> 00:07:43,825
[SPEAKER_01]: Like knowing the presence of hindrances, for example, that's how you understand the way out of them.

106
00:07:44,486 --> 00:07:46,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like they never arise on the level of the body.

107
00:07:49,773 --> 00:07:57,039
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just whatever arises on that level of the body, like whatever is being experienced, it cannot restrict you anymore.

108
00:07:57,179 --> 00:08:05,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you know the escape... Well, yeah, because the only reason it was restricting you is because you restricted yourself by giving in to getting... trying to get rid of craving against the unpleasant feeling.

109
00:08:06,486 --> 00:08:11,670
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the hindrances trap you, but not by like... by their own effort.

110
00:08:12,191 --> 00:08:17,275
[SPEAKER_01]: They like set up traps and you keep stepping into it, by giving in to craving against the discomfort.

111
00:08:18,268 --> 00:08:19,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving for the comfort.

112
00:08:20,289 --> 00:08:22,930
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you trap yourself within the domain of hindrances.

113
00:08:23,550 --> 00:08:28,412
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you trap yourself within these random shapes of the mind that arise, with this random weather.

114
00:08:28,993 --> 00:08:29,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Literally like weather.

115
00:08:30,513 --> 00:08:34,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind, emotions, if you want to call it, even moods, are the weather.

116
00:08:34,915 --> 00:08:40,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes it's sunny, sometimes it's hot, sometimes cold, sometimes windy, sometimes it rains.

117
00:08:40,197 --> 00:08:41,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Neither of those weather

118
00:08:42,243 --> 00:08:50,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Circumstances, conditions, need to be affecting how you... like, your responses, your feelings, your thoughts and what you're trying to deal with.

119
00:08:50,928 --> 00:08:51,068
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

120
00:08:51,669 --> 00:08:53,530
[SPEAKER_01]: It could make no difference if you go beyond it.

121
00:08:53,610 --> 00:08:54,570
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't mind if it's rain.

122
00:08:54,650 --> 00:08:55,531
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't mind if it's sunny.

123
00:08:56,852 --> 00:08:58,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I remain untouched by that.

124
00:08:58,633 --> 00:09:00,274
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you're not excited or depressed.

125
00:09:00,394 --> 00:09:00,774
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

126
00:09:00,914 --> 00:09:03,496
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't wish for rain and cry when it's too hot.

127
00:09:04,036 --> 00:09:07,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Nor do I cry when it's raining and get all depressed when things are wet.

128
00:09:09,285 --> 00:09:12,107
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you experience a disagreeable thing... Hm.

129
00:09:12,627 --> 00:09:13,487
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just disagreeable.

130
00:09:13,527 --> 00:09:14,108
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't get all...

131
00:09:14,128 --> 00:09:15,829
[SPEAKER_01]: So, latent mind is known as a latent mind.

132
00:09:15,849 --> 00:09:17,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Depressed mind is a depressed mind.

133
00:09:17,710 --> 00:09:18,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Neither latent nor depressed.

134
00:09:18,950 --> 00:09:21,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Neither latent nor depressed.

135
00:09:22,012 --> 00:09:25,874
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in a sense, the having emotions... Hm.

136
00:09:29,111 --> 00:09:31,412
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a symptom, yeah.

137
00:09:31,692 --> 00:09:32,553
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a symptom.

138
00:09:32,713 --> 00:09:43,240
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't need to deal with that, but you need to start looking what else is there beyond it, that is the more of a problem than these emotions that are a symptom of a problem.

139
00:09:44,021 --> 00:09:46,822
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the... like emotions, it's literally where the comes.

140
00:09:47,323 --> 00:09:47,963
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't stop it.

141
00:09:47,983 --> 00:09:50,405
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not because you've done something wrong and it started to rain.

142
00:09:52,937 --> 00:10:00,262
[SPEAKER_01]: But you want to see, well, is there anything that I am doing that's responsible for me being affected by these emotions, by this weather, by these moods that come and go?

143
00:10:01,183 --> 00:10:02,163
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, yeah, there is.

144
00:10:02,664 --> 00:10:07,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you'll be affected by rain only when you crave for doing things on account of sunny, good weather.

145
00:10:08,087 --> 00:10:08,948
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't really mind...

146
00:10:12,489 --> 00:10:15,570
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not be affected by when the weather changes, because it doesn't affect you.

147
00:10:15,670 --> 00:10:27,075
[SPEAKER_01]: You were not committed to emotionally, to things you want to do, for the sake of comfort, pleasure, which require sunshine, which require good weather, which cannot be done in rain.

148
00:10:29,296 --> 00:10:39,540
[SPEAKER_01]: But say, alternatively, if you're a bit tired of work, tired of people, you want rain, because that everything stops, everything goes quiet, nobody comes, nobody goes anywhere.

149
00:10:40,453 --> 00:10:41,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, then you would want to rain.

150
00:10:41,954 --> 00:10:46,679
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when the sun comes, people wake up and start going left and right and coming to your place.

151
00:10:47,439 --> 00:10:48,240
[SPEAKER_01]: You will not want that.

152
00:10:50,662 --> 00:10:54,406
[SPEAKER_01]: So, on some occasion, the same weather makes you happy, makes you sad.

153
00:10:54,586 --> 00:10:55,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

154
00:10:55,166 --> 00:10:56,167
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not because of the weather.

155
00:10:56,207 --> 00:11:03,694
[SPEAKER_01]: If things were truly rooted in the weather, you would always be happy when it's a certain weather, when it's a certain shape of the mind, when it's a certain state of the mind.

156
00:11:03,834 --> 00:11:04,355
[SPEAKER_01]: But you weren't.

157
00:11:06,867 --> 00:11:11,310
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're happy based on basically where your craving has projected your expectations, pretty much.

158
00:11:11,410 --> 00:11:15,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving rooted in how you feel and how you want to avoid feeling.

159
00:11:19,075 --> 00:11:21,577
[SPEAKER_00]: So, getting emotional.

160
00:11:23,910 --> 00:11:29,352
[SPEAKER_00]: It can show you in which direction you need to be questioning things.

161
00:11:29,712 --> 00:11:31,232
[SPEAKER_01]: It shows you what the problem is.

162
00:11:31,252 --> 00:11:32,412
[SPEAKER_00]: You're liable to emotions.

163
00:11:32,512 --> 00:11:33,693
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you have a problem.

164
00:11:33,753 --> 00:11:41,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Emotions would not have arisen without the necessary basis of presently enduring craving for that, against, or for that certain feeling that's there.

165
00:11:42,415 --> 00:11:43,035
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's say...

166
00:11:43,116 --> 00:11:51,598
[SPEAKER_01]: If you got emotional, that can only be done on the level of... it can be done on the basis of you craving against this pleasure or pleasure.

167
00:11:53,172 --> 00:11:54,073
[SPEAKER_01]: You're craving for pleasure.

168
00:12:01,798 --> 00:12:07,482
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once that craving is removed or diminished...

169
00:12:07,522 --> 00:12:07,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Diminished.

170
00:12:08,102 --> 00:12:08,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Gradually, yeah.

171
00:12:08,882 --> 00:12:09,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Gradually diminished.

172
00:12:09,483 --> 00:12:10,664
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be less and less emotional.

173
00:12:10,844 --> 00:12:11,724
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be cooling down.

174
00:12:12,585 --> 00:12:12,905
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

175
00:12:13,606 --> 00:12:16,908
[SPEAKER_01]: So... You can't be emotional, by the way, without having passion.

176
00:12:18,506 --> 00:12:23,331
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can regard emotions as a symptom, or you can regard a passion as a symptom.

177
00:12:27,176 --> 00:12:35,625
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can say, if there is craving in regard to how you're feeling, whatever you're feeling at the time, present and enduring, there is passion, there is emotion.

178
00:12:36,946 --> 00:12:37,627
[SPEAKER_01]: To what extent?

179
00:12:37,667 --> 00:12:40,470
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's determined by the extent of your ignorance of your own craving.

180
00:12:43,398 --> 00:12:50,660
[SPEAKER_01]: It's determined by the extent you took your own feelings for granted, as something to be wished for, delighted in, or something to be resisted, and got rid of.

181
00:12:55,120 --> 00:13:03,525
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so for... like being emotion... emotionless seems to be... yeah, that's probably a fear of most.

182
00:13:03,545 --> 00:13:10,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you don't just want to be depressed, looking... Well, yeah, because from their point of view... ...dead inside.

183
00:13:10,129 --> 00:13:21,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the idea... any idea of emotionlessness is judged by, is discerned, is discriminated against the background of what they know, which is emotions.

184
00:13:23,307 --> 00:13:33,219
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from the point of view of emotions, it's like... it feels like... from the point of view of passion, being passion-free, passionless, it's like... well, that's like death from the point of view of passion.

185
00:13:34,801 --> 00:13:36,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it is from the point of view of passion.

186
00:13:36,864 --> 00:13:39,086
[SPEAKER_00]: That is who you are.

187
00:13:39,206 --> 00:13:41,129
[SPEAKER_01]: It is death of the passion which you are.

188
00:13:41,629 --> 00:13:42,550
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's death of you.

189
00:13:43,585 --> 00:13:44,506
[SPEAKER_01]: The way you know yourself.

190
00:13:44,986 --> 00:13:45,666
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have a choice.

191
00:13:45,686 --> 00:13:57,734
[SPEAKER_01]: You can either practice towards the death of passion, or you can stay out of fear, not go there, and then be liable to everything else that passion is basically direct cause of.

192
00:13:57,774 --> 00:14:05,979
[SPEAKER_01]: The entire saṃsara of suffering, liability, torment, despair, lamentation, death, the real death.

193
00:14:08,601 --> 00:14:09,782
[SPEAKER_01]: So, because you're afraid to die,

194
00:14:11,003 --> 00:14:16,826
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're afraid to let your passions and emotions die, you're bound to actually die.

195
00:14:17,826 --> 00:14:18,927
[SPEAKER_01]: You bind yourself to death.

196
00:14:21,048 --> 00:14:22,969
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're afraid of it.

197
00:14:26,851 --> 00:14:29,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's how you reach deathless, as the Buddha would say.

198
00:14:29,372 --> 00:14:32,393
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how death does not apply to you anymore.

199
00:14:32,413 --> 00:14:34,094
[SPEAKER_00]: By extinguishing passions and emotions.

200
00:14:40,450 --> 00:14:44,191
It's not like that.

201
00:14:44,291 --> 00:14:45,051
It's not like that.

202
00:15:07,736 --> 00:15:10,681
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you should not act out of your emotions.

203
00:15:10,761 --> 00:15:12,624
[SPEAKER_00]: You should not act out of emotions.

204
00:15:12,644 --> 00:15:13,265
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's the first step.

205
00:15:13,385 --> 00:15:14,787
[SPEAKER_01]: You should not act out of emotions.

206
00:15:14,807 --> 00:15:20,596
[SPEAKER_01]: You should become aware of them, while they're enduring, especially.

207
00:15:22,020 --> 00:15:23,221
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you don't act out of them.

208
00:15:23,922 --> 00:15:27,925
[SPEAKER_01]: But you should also see them as a symptom of an underlying problem.

209
00:15:28,385 --> 00:15:35,211
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're not going to see that... Not think that, oh, like, just being aware of it and enduring it somehow, I'm dealing with it.

210
00:15:35,271 --> 00:15:36,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they were not a problem.

211
00:15:36,612 --> 00:15:43,918
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you think that the symptoms are the problem, you're not going to look for what the real problem is, the cause of these symptoms.

212
00:15:43,938 --> 00:15:51,504
[SPEAKER_01]: So, pretty much, unless you're free from suffering, unless you understood the Four Noble Truths and became Sotāpanna, everything you think is a problem, is really a symptom of a problem.

213
00:15:53,578 --> 00:15:54,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

214
00:15:55,820 --> 00:15:59,203
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, when you suffer, suffering is a symptom of liability to suffering.

215
00:16:00,884 --> 00:16:04,587
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

216
00:16:04,828 --> 00:16:05,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what the problem is.

217
00:16:05,948 --> 00:16:08,591
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you suffer in the first place, because you were liable to suffer.

218
00:16:09,632 --> 00:16:09,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

219
00:16:09,872 --> 00:16:10,572
[SPEAKER_00]: That's dukkha.

220
00:16:10,752 --> 00:16:12,914
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what dukkha is, yeah.

221
00:16:12,934 --> 00:16:14,115
[SPEAKER_00]: The symptoms of that.

222
00:16:15,336 --> 00:16:16,858
[SPEAKER_01]: Suffering is the symptom of dukkha.

223
00:16:18,199 --> 00:16:19,380
[SPEAKER_01]: And dukkha is the problem.

224
00:16:20,461 --> 00:16:25,604
[SPEAKER_01]: So, freedom from dukkha then means freedom from symptoms of dukkha, which is freedom from suffering.

225
00:16:27,526 --> 00:16:28,787
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an unfortunate translation.

226
00:16:28,987 --> 00:16:35,791
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it kind of... yeah, you could say that because symptoms of suffering... symptoms of dukkha are suffering, dukkha is also suffering.

227
00:16:35,871 --> 00:16:36,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

228
00:16:37,092 --> 00:16:38,293
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not on the same level.

229
00:16:39,254 --> 00:16:40,775
[SPEAKER_01]: Dukkha, that needs to be understood,

230
00:16:43,792 --> 00:16:49,319
[SPEAKER_01]: I am subject to suffering.

231
00:16:49,419 --> 00:16:50,881
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm subject to misfortune.

232
00:16:51,101 --> 00:16:53,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, nothing has happened to me, but it could.

233
00:16:54,345 --> 00:16:57,589
[SPEAKER_01]: I am subjected to the possibility of these things happening.

234
00:16:57,870 --> 00:16:59,532
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm a prey to suffering, exactly.

235
00:17:02,220 --> 00:17:09,484
[SPEAKER_01]: But if people just confuse dukkha to be the suffering that's just felt, sometimes felt, sometimes isn't, they're not looking for what the real problem is.

236
00:17:09,984 --> 00:17:13,846
[SPEAKER_01]: So, literally, then, the problem is only when you're being attacked by a lion.

237
00:17:14,406 --> 00:17:18,208
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't recognize that the problem is that the lion can pounce on you at any given time.

238
00:17:18,328 --> 00:17:19,189
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already the problem.

239
00:17:19,569 --> 00:17:22,511
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the only reason why the lion eventually did attack you.

240
00:17:22,751 --> 00:17:26,753
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you remained within the lion's boundaries, where the lion hunts.

241
00:17:27,786 --> 00:17:35,313
[SPEAKER_01]: But see, even before the lion attacks you, if you recognize that this is the hunting ground of a lion, means I am liable to it.

242
00:17:36,414 --> 00:17:39,037
[SPEAKER_01]: I better do something about it before lion attacks me.

243
00:17:39,956 --> 00:17:40,957
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what dukkha is.

244
00:17:41,477 --> 00:17:42,698
[SPEAKER_01]: That recognition of that.

245
00:17:42,718 --> 00:17:45,059
[SPEAKER_01]: So, literally, dukkha means unease.

246
00:17:45,719 --> 00:17:47,500
[SPEAKER_01]: Like sukha means ease.

247
00:17:47,961 --> 00:17:48,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Dukkha is the opposite.

248
00:17:48,881 --> 00:17:50,542
[SPEAKER_01]: It's dis-ease, unease.

249
00:17:51,082 --> 00:17:51,663
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly...

250
00:17:51,683 --> 00:17:52,623
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's nothing wrong with me.

251
00:17:52,783 --> 00:17:54,484
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not being mauled by a lion, yet.

252
00:17:55,265 --> 00:18:00,648
[SPEAKER_01]: But I recognize that this is literally the stomping ground of wild cats that hunt people.

253
00:18:00,668 --> 00:18:01,728
[SPEAKER_01]: I am prey to that.

254
00:18:02,509 --> 00:18:04,550
[SPEAKER_01]: The recognition of that will make you very uneasy.

255
00:18:06,379 --> 00:18:13,927
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's much easier to just ignore that, because anxiety, fear, dread... Oh, how do I deal with this?

256
00:18:13,947 --> 00:18:17,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can't just hide, because you're still within the same hunting ground.

257
00:18:17,891 --> 00:18:21,675
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to step outside eventually, and you still step outside into the area where they will kill you.

258
00:18:23,524 --> 00:18:37,136
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's easier to ignore, then, that... that anxious recognition of that uneasiness, that you are prey to suffering of any kind, and then just focus on the present moment, whatever's presently arisen, and with wishful thinking, that everything will work out.

259
00:18:38,698 --> 00:18:42,021
[SPEAKER_01]: So, literally, then, you wait for things to arise in order to practice.

260
00:18:43,713 --> 00:18:44,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'm feeling this right now.

261
00:18:45,076 --> 00:18:46,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the feeling has come.

262
00:18:46,418 --> 00:18:47,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Now the feeling has passed away.

263
00:18:47,681 --> 00:18:51,148
[SPEAKER_01]: As if passing away the feeling meant you dealt with it somehow.

264
00:18:51,188 --> 00:18:51,268
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

265
00:18:51,288 --> 00:18:54,454
[SPEAKER_01]: It means, okay, so you evaded an attack of a lion.

266
00:18:55,372 --> 00:18:57,773
[SPEAKER_01]: Does that mean now you're not liable to another attack?

267
00:18:57,893 --> 00:18:59,214
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

268
00:18:59,234 --> 00:19:03,016
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you were attacked by a lion, you got away, you recognized that that was suffering.

269
00:19:03,036 --> 00:19:03,717
[SPEAKER_00]: Got away, yeah.

270
00:19:04,197 --> 00:19:06,378
[SPEAKER_00]: And you keep evading, evading, evading.

271
00:19:06,598 --> 00:19:08,599
[SPEAKER_00]: And you say, well, I don't suffer as much.

272
00:19:08,859 --> 00:19:09,720
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly.

273
00:19:09,820 --> 00:19:13,122
[SPEAKER_01]: I must be... Yeah, I became very skilled in hiding away from a lion.

274
00:19:13,242 --> 00:19:13,502
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

275
00:19:14,222 --> 00:19:17,464
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but the only reason you have to do that is because you're still within the lion's grounds.

276
00:19:17,764 --> 00:19:22,867
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the Buddha's practice takes you out of the dukkha, takes you out of the lion's grounds.

277
00:19:24,243 --> 00:19:26,104
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you don't need to... that's true burden.

278
00:19:26,124 --> 00:19:32,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, see, you might be skillful at evading the lion, but you're constantly burdened by the duty of... you need to hide.

279
00:19:33,508 --> 00:19:35,429
[SPEAKER_01]: One slip, one error, you're done.

280
00:19:36,069 --> 00:19:39,872
[SPEAKER_01]: And, bottom line is, deep down, you know, eventually, it will have to get you.

281
00:19:40,972 --> 00:19:41,813
[SPEAKER_01]: It will get you.

282
00:19:41,833 --> 00:19:43,554
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the divine messengers.

283
00:19:44,294 --> 00:19:46,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Sickness, aging, you can't hide from that.

284
00:19:46,255 --> 00:19:49,637
[SPEAKER_01]: You can hide from everything else in between, semi-successfully.

285
00:19:50,633 --> 00:19:59,615
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if your practice never makes you realize, oh, it's about liability to suffering, being prey to suffering, as the Buddha would say, you're not practicing.

286
00:20:00,195 --> 00:20:01,035
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just managing.

287
00:20:02,096 --> 00:20:03,016
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not the practice.

288
00:20:03,536 --> 00:20:04,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Practice uproots things.

289
00:20:05,597 --> 00:20:06,637
[SPEAKER_01]: Practice doesn't manage.

290
00:20:08,917 --> 00:20:09,858
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, so...

291
00:20:10,578 --> 00:20:19,720
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the fact that you go throughout your day without experiencing much lust, without experiencing much anger, without experiencing much suffering of any kind,

292
00:20:20,586 --> 00:20:22,769
[SPEAKER_01]: It means nothing.

293
00:20:22,869 --> 00:20:28,876
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it because you have stepped outside of the domain, where you were a prey to these things?

294
00:20:28,976 --> 00:20:36,765
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, is it because it's impossible for you to suffer, because you're not liable to future lust, aversion, delusion, or it's simply because circumstances suit you?

295
00:20:37,986 --> 00:20:38,547
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one is it?

296
00:20:38,787 --> 00:20:41,030
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you look at it honestly, you already know the answer.

297
00:20:42,507 --> 00:20:46,988
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's easy to think that, well, see, since I discovered Buddhism, I don't suffer as much.

298
00:20:47,048 --> 00:20:47,568
[SPEAKER_01]: But why is that?

299
00:20:47,588 --> 00:20:52,869
[SPEAKER_01]: Because I understood the nature of that dis-ease, that uneasy, that pray to suffering of any kind.

300
00:20:53,650 --> 00:21:01,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Or because, you know, I'm a bit more careful in what I do or say, practice restraint and live in this protected spiritual community or the environment.

301
00:21:01,691 --> 00:21:02,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Which one is it?

302
00:21:03,172 --> 00:21:07,253
[SPEAKER_01]: So, am I internally free from suffering or just external, have external buffer zones?

303
00:21:09,175 --> 00:21:12,816
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, if you're honest, the answer will be quite simple and obvious.

304
00:21:12,856 --> 00:21:13,596
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already there.

305
00:21:14,276 --> 00:21:19,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you know internally that you're free, you're an outsider, you know how to escape the domain of lying.

306
00:21:19,657 --> 00:21:21,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Not even a specific lying.

307
00:21:21,338 --> 00:21:26,159
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would you worry about specific lying, which might jump on you, when you don't live where the lions hunt?

308
00:21:26,659 --> 00:21:32,700
[SPEAKER_00]: So, keeping that image of lying, being inside the lying enclosure, so to speak.

309
00:21:33,201 --> 00:21:33,401
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

310
00:21:33,501 --> 00:21:34,321
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can...

311
00:21:35,341 --> 00:21:36,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Game reserve.

312
00:21:36,162 --> 00:21:38,223
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

313
00:21:39,304 --> 00:21:48,050
[SPEAKER_00]: And you can describe all the ways that a lion can maul you, or the lion can catch you, and you can recognize all the different ways that you can suffer.

314
00:21:48,651 --> 00:21:52,834
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can feel enriched on account of it, but fundamentally nothing has changed.

315
00:21:52,854 --> 00:21:53,274
[SPEAKER_00]: You're still...

316
00:21:53,294 --> 00:21:57,977
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter if you know which... how many ways lions can sort of drag you around and kill you.

317
00:21:58,118 --> 00:22:01,000
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is you're still liable and you're going to... and subjected.

318
00:22:01,907 --> 00:22:05,609
[SPEAKER_01]: To the possibility of one of those ways applying to you.

319
00:22:06,189 --> 00:22:09,410
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not about study, studying the ways you can suffer.

320
00:22:09,891 --> 00:22:14,733
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about recognizing that you can suffer, full stop, on account of any way.

321
00:22:16,514 --> 00:22:19,575
[SPEAKER_01]: See, what is... That's why dhamma is the nature of things.

322
00:22:20,055 --> 00:22:23,156
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not this specific manifestation of that, that's within the nature.

323
00:22:23,557 --> 00:22:26,918
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to understand how to be free from these things, you need to understand the nature of it.

324
00:22:26,938 --> 00:22:30,860
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is the nature of you being a prey to lions hunting?

325
00:22:32,329 --> 00:22:35,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the nature is that you are within the lion's domain.

326
00:22:36,993 --> 00:22:40,397
[SPEAKER_01]: Can lions get you if you're not within their enclosure?

327
00:22:41,358 --> 00:22:42,979
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're outside of their game reserve?

328
00:22:43,920 --> 00:22:44,881
[SPEAKER_01]: If you abandon it?

329
00:22:44,941 --> 00:22:46,503
[SPEAKER_01]: If you freed yourself from it?

330
00:22:46,643 --> 00:22:47,363
[SPEAKER_01]: If you broke away?

331
00:22:47,644 --> 00:22:49,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible for lions to hunt you down.

332
00:22:49,746 --> 00:22:51,647
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why are you in that enclosure?

333
00:22:52,368 --> 00:22:54,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, because you don't recognize that you are prey to suffering.

334
00:22:55,566 --> 00:22:56,786
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't recognize that you're a prey.

335
00:22:57,087 --> 00:23:00,748
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't recognize that you're a prey, because that's... it freaks people out, when you recognize that.

336
00:23:01,448 --> 00:23:04,789
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why people would freak out, when the Buddha would teach them, and the minds were not ready.

337
00:23:05,950 --> 00:23:10,692
[SPEAKER_00]: But rec... so, recognizing that you are a prey... Basically, ignoring what the real problem is.

338
00:23:10,712 --> 00:23:13,232
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you remain under that problem.

339
00:23:14,293 --> 00:23:19,875
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the problem is that you are liable to suffering, not what specifically, or on account of what specific thing you suffer.

340
00:23:20,255 --> 00:23:22,416
[SPEAKER_00]: But... but understand that you somehow...

341
00:23:23,959 --> 00:23:25,600
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you no longer be a prey?

342
00:23:26,220 --> 00:23:28,100
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you no longer be a prey to the lion?

343
00:23:30,041 --> 00:23:30,941
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you no longer be?

344
00:23:31,202 --> 00:23:34,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, by understanding, like, what the extent of the lion's domain.

345
00:23:34,983 --> 00:23:39,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding the extent of the dukkha, you will understand what the freedom from that, what the escape from that dukkha is.

346
00:23:40,825 --> 00:23:49,088
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, the the truth that you will discover, the four noble truths, basically, but let's keep with the simile, would be, oh, the only reason

347
00:23:50,494 --> 00:23:57,260
[SPEAKER_01]: The lions can hunt you down and will eventually, is because you yourself keep yourself in that game reserve.

348
00:24:03,326 --> 00:24:07,190
[SPEAKER_00]: And you keep yourself there because you... Well, because you value pleasures that you get there.

349
00:24:08,751 --> 00:24:09,732
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, it has nice fruits.

350
00:24:11,634 --> 00:24:12,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Good company.

351
00:24:13,972 --> 00:24:14,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Beautiful views.

352
00:24:14,913 --> 00:24:15,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

353
00:24:15,353 --> 00:24:15,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Adventure.

354
00:24:15,874 --> 00:24:16,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Lovely streams.

355
00:24:16,914 --> 00:24:17,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

356
00:24:17,675 --> 00:24:20,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever basically gets you going, it has it.

357
00:24:20,417 --> 00:24:24,941
[SPEAKER_01]: But at the same time, it has lions that are stalking you and hunting you down.

358
00:24:25,141 --> 00:24:25,361
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

359
00:24:27,703 --> 00:24:28,784
[SPEAKER_00]: So, no longer...

360
00:24:29,244 --> 00:24:41,474
[SPEAKER_01]: If you were to not forget that there are lions might be watching you at any given time, no amount of these joyful pleasures that that game deserve can offer, will be enough for you to stay there.

361
00:24:42,644 --> 00:24:48,111
[SPEAKER_01]: But, what happens is, most of the time, you don't see the lions.

362
00:24:49,333 --> 00:24:53,658
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you afford to forget that you are still a prey to the lions, even if you don't see them.

363
00:24:54,359 --> 00:25:00,487
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by affording to forget that, you then afford to be attracted and appreciate all these beauties of senses that you can get from that game result.

364
00:25:02,891 --> 00:25:06,253
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's literally forgetting that you're liable to death.

365
00:25:07,314 --> 00:25:10,176
[SPEAKER_01]: Maraṇasati, it's exactly that.

366
00:25:10,436 --> 00:25:12,978
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't see death, so you don't think about it.

367
00:25:13,038 --> 00:25:16,961
[SPEAKER_01]: By not thinking about it, you maintain the whole world of sensuality, anger, despair, everything else.

368
00:25:19,043 --> 00:25:31,972
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you were not to forget that you're liable to die at any given second, and in-breath, out-breath, as the Buddha would say, any attraction you have on account of being alive, any passion, will have to disappear.

369
00:25:32,905 --> 00:25:35,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Eventually, it will wither away, because you're not maintaining it anymore.

